// REGRESSION GUARD (was a SIGSEGV reproducer; FIXED at HEAD 2026-06-18).
// STATUS: now prints 25, ec=0, both --mode vm and --mode jit. PASS.
//
// HISTORICAL: on the prior acceptance pass this crashed with SIGSEGV (ec=139,
// no diagnostic) under both modes. A named function passed as a higher-order-
// function argument and captured into a RETURNED closure, then invoked, used to
// crash. It now evaluates correctly. Kept as a regression guard.
//
// Book (fundamentals/functions.mdx) documents the constituent pieces as working:
//   - §Higher-Order Functions: "A function whose parameter is itself a function
//     can take a lambda directly" + apply(double, 21).
//   - §Higher-Order Functions: "Returning a callable and binding it to a `let`
//     works" (adder).
// The composition (named fn -> captured into returned closure -> called) crashes.
//
// CONTRAST: wrap(dbl) where dbl is a *lambda* works and prints 10.

fn square(x: int) -> int { x * x }
fn wrap(f) { |x| f(x) }

let w = wrap(square)
print(w(5))   // expected 25; actual: SIGSEGV (ec=139)
